WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.033
Choosing a local vision model is an engineering decision, not a popularity contest.

00:00:07.033 --> 00:00:14.552
Vision-language models differ across OCR, objects, counting, charts, screenshots, descriptions, and visual question answering.

00:00:14.552 --> 00:00:18.674
The use case defines which differences matter.

00:00:18.674 --> 00:00:26.678
Hardware fit includes VRAM or unified memory, system RAM, accelerator support, concurrency, and operational headroom.

00:00:26.678 --> 00:00:34.681
Model size, quantization, image-token budget, runtime, prompt template, and thinking mode form one tested configuration.

00:00:34.681 --> 00:00:44.625
Accuracy must be considered with completion, latency, memory failures, hallucinations, OCR and counting errors, and consistency across repeated runs.

00:00:44.625 --> 00:00:54.083
A smaller or lower-scoring model can be better when it is stable and fast enough for the workflow.

00:00:54.083 --> 00:01:02.571
Benchmark locally using representative images, prewritten rubrics, repeated runs, controlled settings, and a durable evaluation log.

00:01:02.571 --> 00:01:08.150
Community benchmarks provide hypotheses and hardware tiers, not permanent answers.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:15.183
Deployment adds privacy, licensing, retention, cost, power, monitoring, fallback, updates, and human review.

00:01:15.183 --> 00:01:19.306
Keep important decisions grounded in visible evidence.

00:01:19.306 --> 00:01:30.704
The best model is the configuration that meets task quality and reliability on available hardware while preserving privacy and accountable human judgment.
